 same way: it wants /h0/GAMES/larn/PLAYGROUND, andGAMES/LARN/PLAYGROUND is on the disk.  It holds the scoreboard larnwrites there for itself, plus .larn.help, .lfortune and .larnmaze.larn plays: it lays out a level, shows its '?' help, keeps score andtakes commands.Those three data files came from the larn 12.2p4 sources; the binaryreports 12.0.  SOURCES.txt records where they came from.  Its siblingularn is complete and also runs.  gnuchessc and gnuan read their datafrom GNUCHESS4.0/MISC and both start.DOC/DEPENDS lists, per program, every absolute path its binarycarries -- read it before copying single commands elsewhere.See DOC/REBUILT-NOTES.md for four curated binaries that wereoverwritten during installation and what was done about each.------------------------------------------------------/dd/CMDS -- added utilities (356)------------------------------------------------------  agrep          grep that forgives spelling: `agrep -2 homogenos file' finds                 `homogeneous', allowing up to two letters wrong, missing                 or extra.  -i ignores case, -w matches whole words, -c                 counts, -f takes many patterns from a file, and -d splits                 the text into records (`-d "^From "' for a mailbox)  almanac        computes where the Sun, Moon and the eight planets are for a                 given date and time: right ascension, declination, apparent                 diameter and distance in AU.  Add your longitude and latitude                 and it also gives azimuth and altitude.  Its constants were                 taken from The Astronomical Almanac 1990, so accuracy softens                 the further you go from then  anagram        finds the anagrams of a word in the word list GAMES/words:                 `anagram listen' prints enlist, listen, silent and tinsel;                 -l also lists near misses with their leftover letters  bc             an arbitrary-precision calculator: the num