our own shell loaded -- DOC/README-SHELLS *make           make -- maintains a target. Two rules catch people: a                 command line must begin with a TAB (which will not survive                 being typed at this terminal, so copy DOC/make/demo.mk                 rather than echoing one), and a recipe must have no shell                 metacharacter -- `cp a b' runs, `cat a > b' gets `That path                 name doesn't lead to a file'.  DOC/STATUS has both                 Shares its name with a utility of your own -- README-NAMES *makelex        compiles sonnet's lex.data word list into a C array  marc           the arc archive merger -- `marc <target> <source> [names]'                 copies members from one .arc into another *mexist         answers whether a module is in the module directory by its                 exit status rather than by printing: 0 if it is there, 1 if                 it is not. The name is case-sensitive, and it looks at up to                 256 modules *mg             Mg, a small MicroGnuEmacs -- Emacs keys in 79K, its                 documentation under DOC/mg. *minnesota      the weather program on its north-Atlantic profile; see england *mkdir          makes a directory; -p makes every directory on the way to                 it, -m sets its mode  more           the plain pager: a screenful at a time, Enter for the next.                 The screenful is the terminal's page length (tmode pag=);                 at 0 it never stops.  It reads Enter from standard input,                 so it cannot page a pipe -- use less for that *move           move files between directories without copying the contents                 -- it relinks them, which is why it is quick and why its own                 help warns never to kill it mid-run. `move <from> <to>'                 wants a destination name; -w=<dir> is the wildcard form that                 takes a directory. *mtst           exercises the C maths l