ts.  uuexpand       expands a file into a run of `0' and `1' characters, one per                 bit -- despite the shared prefix, unrelated to uuencode --                 so it survives a copy between machines with different byte                 or character sizes; `uuexpand -u' (or `uuunexpand') reverses                 it *version        prints its own version and nothing else -- `Dies ist das                 Program 'version', Version 7' -- whatever module you name.                 `modinfo' shows a module's edition, and your own OS-9's                 `ident' reports what a module was built from                 On a real terminal it works and is used daily.  `vi' and                 REBUILT/vi_1.0 are the other vi editors here  vis            run a command over and over and refresh the screen with                 its output -- what `watch' does on other systems:                 `vis {opts} <command> <args>'.  Not the Unix `vis' that                 makes non-printing characters visible  wam.sbprolog   SB-Prolog 2.2, a full Prolog. Needs                 SIMPATH=/dd/SBPROLOG/MODLIB; `wam.sbprolog                 SBPROLOG/MODLIB/$readloop' from /dd gives the `?-' prompt,                 and halt. leaves. DOC/sbprolog has the manual and README-                 SBPROLOG  vttest         the VT100 compatibility test: a menu of pages for cursor                 movement, screen features, character sets, double-size                 lines, the keyboard, status reports, VT52 mode and VT102                 editing, each saying what a correct terminal shows.  0                 leaves  weekday        the day of the week, julian day and week number of a date:                 `weekday 10/30/89' says Monday, day 303, week 44  whetstone      the Whetstone benchmark: floating-point work in a fixed mix,                 timed, and the rating printed in Whetstone instructions per                 second.  The Dhrystone builds in CMDS/DHRY measure integer