are.                 Like UniBasic it needs Microware's cc to finish a build  ub68020demo    UniBasic 1.10 for the 68020 -- the same demonstration as                 `ub68kdemo' and it runs the same way, announcing `OS9/68020                 Version' where the other says 68000.  sddemo         White's Speedisk 2.10 -- disk de-fragmenter.  Wants an                 80x24 screen; falls back to tty mode  ub68kdemo      UniBasic 1.10 -- a BASIC compiler, same arrangement as                 OmniBasic.  Run it from /dd/DOC/unibasic  Compiling with UniBasic, start to finish.  It works; here is what it  wants.  UniBasic translates your BASIC into C, then drives the C  compiler to build a module, so it needs your OS-9 C toolchain:    These four steps are in the OS-9 shell's own language -- `setenv'    and `chd' are its commands, not bash's.  In bash use `export    NAME=value', and note that bash's `cd' does not move the OS-9    data directory at all (DOC/README-RUNNING says why).    1. Set SHELL.  UniBasic runs its compile through $SHELL.  With SHELL       unset it looks for `/dd/bash' and stops with `Error Exit' and       error 216, so set it:       setenv SHELL /dd/CMDS/sh    2. Work where basic.h and basic.l are.  chd to a directory holding       both, or copy them beside your program.  DOC/unibasic has them.    3. Point at your C toolchain.  cc, c68, o68, r68, l68, cpp and merge       on the execution path; setenv CDEF to your headers and CLIB to       your libraries.  The link wants cstart.r, clibn.l and sys.l.    4. Give it room.  The preprocessor says `No more memory !!!' if it       is short; os9exec users want something like -M 128M -mm 8M.  A five-line program written from scratch, compiled by ub68kdemo, ran  and printed its output.  The compiled  bench1..bench11 programs ship beside their sources, so there is  something to run before you set any of this up.  bio, blackjack and wysetime are BASIC09 I-code, and want runb.---------