 a saved game  The -i is not optional for anything that includes standard.adl,  which is most of it: the include names it without a path, so adlcomp  needs telling which directory to look in, and it takes absolute  paths throughout:      adlcomp /dd/ADL/DEMOS/tiny.adl -o /dd/tmp/tiny -i/dd/ADL      adlrun /dd/tmp/tiny  -- 0 errors, 2 warnings, a 10548-byte world, and it opens in a small  but comfortable room with two pillows in it.  AARD is already built  and needs none of that: `adlrun /dd/ADL/AARD/aard'.  adlcomp       compile an ADL world  adldebug       the ADL debugger: loads a compiled world with its symbol                 table and dumps its tables -- objects, nouns, verbs,                 routines, strings -- over a range of numbers. ? lists the                 commands, q leaves  adlrun         plays a compiled ADL world at a > prompt -- look, take,                 inventory, a direction; save and restore keep a game, quit                 leaves. AARD is ready to run in ADL/AARD: `adlrun aard'  adltouch       re-dates an ADL save file: `adltouch <save> <n>' writes n                 over the first four bytes, the stamp that ties a saved game                 to the world it was saved from -- `touch an ADL save file',                 its source says.  It writes to any file it is given and                 prints nothing------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------/dd/CMDS/NEWS -- C News and rn (12)------------------------------------------------------  C News, Geoff Collyer and Henry Spencer's, University of Toronto,  1985-1990, ported to OS-9 by blarson.  It is the transport and storage  half of Usenet; rn, below, is the newsreader that goes with it.  The archive is called GNU_ATP_1_40.lzh and is not GNU and not ATP; its  own README begins "This is C News, ported to os9/68k by blarson@usc.edu".  Another mislabelled name in that archive.  Terms are permissive: "Permission is granted to anyone to use this  software for any purpose on any computer system, and to alter it and  redistribute it freely", subject to four conditions about credit and not  misrepresenting it.  DOC/cnews/COPYRIGHT is the whole notice, with the  README, ROADMAP and 14 manual pages.  relaynews, C News's relay, files articles into SPOOL/CNEWS; its  configuration is USR/LIB/CNEWS.  rn, Larry Wall's newsreader, reads that  spool, and Pnews posts to it.  One file of each was changed for this  disk's directories (SRC/cnews/README.OSK, SRC/rn43/README.OSK).  newsrun feeds relaynews the batches waiting in SPOOL/CNEWS/in.coming.  It calls blarson's `trlf', which is in no archive; the trlf here was  written for this disk to do the two jobs newsrun gives it.  *bdecode       decodes a C News batch: it skips forward to the line `Decode                 the following with bdecode', decodes what follows and checks                 the CRC at the end. Given anything else it says `Missing                 header'. Source in SRC/cnews/input *byteflip       reorders the bytes of every word it reads on standard input, so a dbz                 database written on one architecture can be read on another.  Four                 things say how: the word length, where each byte comes from, the word                 length again, and where each byte goes -- `byteflip 4 0 1 2 3 4 3 2 1 0'                 turns every four-byte word end for end.  Give it those numbers: with                 none the word length is zero and it reads zero bytes for ever  *c7decode      the inverse of C News's c7encode: it reads the seven-bit-safe                 form a news batch is put into to cross a link that eats the                 eighth bit, and writes the eight-bit original back. Source in                 SRC/cnews/input  *dbz           builds and maintains C News's history index -- the .dir and                 .pag pair beside the history file that lets the news system                 find an article by message-id without reading the whole of                 it. `dbz database [file]...'   newsetup      writes a .newsrc for rn from the groups in the active file,                 each marked unsubscribed until you choose.  rn runs it for a                 newcomer, through your own OS-9's `shell'; run directly it                 needs nothing  *newshist      looks message-ids up in the news history and reports what it                 finds, or that there is no entry for them: `newshist                 "<id@site>" ...'. -df names a history file other than the                 system's *newslock       the news system's lock: `newslock <tempname> <lockname>' makes the lock                 by linking one name to the other -- blarslib's link(), which writes the                 second directory entry itself, since RBF has no hard links.  It returns                 0 and both names share one file, link count 2 (measured 2026-09-23).                 relaynews uses a copying link() instead; see SRC/cnews/README.OSK   newsrun       runs the batches waiting in SPOOL/CNEWS/in.coming through                 relaynews, one by one: uncompresses one that is compressed,                 turns its line endings into CR with trlf, and removes it once                 filed -- a refused batch goes to in.coming/bad.  `newsrun -v'                 says what it does with each.  It runs trlf and compress                 through your own OS-9's `shell', so load trlf first   Pnews         posts an article to C News: it asks for the distribution,                 newsgroup and subject, or with -h <file> -s sends a file that                 has its header.  The post waits in SPOOL/CNEWS/in.coming for                 relaynews   relaynews     C News's relay: files an article or a batch under its                 newsgroup, numbers it, updates the active file and the history,                 and refuses a Message-ID it has seen.  `relay