ows about a user: the home                 directory, the shell, and the .plan it would print.  It                 reads the password file and knows only this machine's                 accounts; there is no network in it, and user@host is                 `No such user'  infoxpress     a client for the InfoXpress information service, reached                 over a serial line  msntp          sets the system clock from a network time server, by SNTP: name the                 server and it asks one.  With no server named it listens for broadcasts                 instead and waits for one, which its own manual (DOC/msntp/msntp.1)                 describes and recommends against -- polling a server is the reliable                 way.  Either way it needs a network to reach, and it loads                 Microware's `netdb' module to look an address up, even a                 dotted number -- your own networking supplies it   bm            the mailer that goes with net: write a letter and it is queued                 for net's SMTP to send; run bare it reads your mail.  It                 reads NETHOME and NETSPOOL as net does.  DOC/bm/bm.doc is                 its manual   net           KA9Q net -- TCP/IP over SLIP or AX.25: telnet, ftp, smtp.                 Set NETHOME (startup.net, hosts.net and the rest) and                 NETSPOOL (mail and its queues); Chapter 2 of DOC/net says                 how, and `exit' leaves it   nslookup      BIND 4.8.3's name server lookup: `nslookup <host>' asks the                 server for a name's addresses, and run bare it takes queries                 at its own prompt.  It reads the server's address from                 resolv.conf at the root of /h0 (an example is in DOC/bind)                 and wants Microware's ISP networking under it   nsquery       BIND 4.8.3's small resolver test: `nsquery <host> [server]'                 prints a host's names and addresses.  Same resolv.conf, same             