  suffixes are optimisation and inlining variants.  Running several on the  same machine tells you what the compiler costs you.  On real hardware this measures your machine.  Run under an emulator it  measures the host instead, which is still worth having if you have a  figure from real hardware to set beside it.  Source is in SRC/dhry.  dhry           Dhrystone 2.0 built with Microware's cc. It reads the number                 of runs from standard input: `echo 500000 | dhry'. Twelve                 builds of the same source sit in CMDS/DHRY, so the compilers                 can be compared *dhryGcc        Dhrystone 2.0 built with GCC 1; see dhry *dhryGcc2       Dhrystone 2.0 built with GCC 2; see dhry *dhryGcc2in     Dhrystone 2.0 built with GCC 2 with inlining; see dhry *dhryGcc2mx     Dhrystone 2.0 built with GCC 2, mixed options; see dhry *dhryGcc2o2     Dhrystone 2.0 built with GCC 2 at -O2; see dhry *dhryGccin      Dhrystone 2.0 built with GCC 1 with inlining; see dhry *dhryGccmx      Dhrystone 2.0 built with GCC 1, mixed options; see dhry *dhryGcco2      Dhrystone 2.0 built with GCC 1 at -O2; see dhry  dhryO2         Dhrystone 2.0 built with Microware's cc at -O2; see dhry *dhryshamu      Dhrystone 2.0, the shamu build; see dhry *dhryshamu2     Dhrystone 2.0, a second shamu build; see dhry------------------------------------------------------/dd/CMDS/GCC139 and /dd/CMDS/GCC2 -- the gcc toolchains (15)------------------------------------------------------Two generations of GNU C for OS-9/68k.  Microware's cc is the compiler foralmost everything on this disk; gcc is here because a few programs needANSI C, which Microware's K&R cc cannot parse.  ls is the one on this diskthat was built this way.Read DOC/README-GCC first.  gcc2 searches /dd/CMDS/gcc_<pass> and gppsearches /dd/CMDS/gpp_<pass>, both hardcoded rather than /dd/CMDS/GCC2, sothe five passes those two drivers look for are installed in /dd/CMDS underthe names they ask for:    `gcc_cccp2'  `gcc_cc2'  `gpp_cccp'  `gpp_cc1plus'  `gpp_collect'They are byte copies of cccp2, cc2, cccp2 again, cc1plus and collect from/dd/CMDS/GCC2 -- cccp2 twice on purpose, because gcc2 forks it as cccp2and gpp forks it as cccp.  The originals stay where they are.  GCC139 isnot installed this way; its gcc_cc1plus and GCC2's would want one name.The programs:  gcc           the GCC driver -- GCC139's and GCC2's share this name.                They are not the same version, and neither is 2.x:                GCC139/gcc answers `gcc version 1.39' and GCC2/gcc                answers `gcc version 1.42', read out of `gcc -v'  gcc2          the GCC 2.x driver, and the only one that is:                `gcc version 2.5.6'  gpp           the C++ driver -- and it is a GCC 1.x one.  GCC2/gpp                says `gpp version 1.40.3 (based on GCC 1.40)' and                GCC139/gpp says 1.37.1  gcc_cc1       GCC 1.39 C compiler pass  gcc_cc1plus   GCC 1.39 C++ compiler pass  gcc_cccp      GCC 1.39 preprocessor  gcc_cccp2     GCC 2.x preprocessor, under the name gcc2 forks  gcc_cc2       GCC 2.x compiler pass, under the name gcc2 forks  gcc_collect   GCC 1.39 collect2  gpp_cccp      GCC 2.x preprocessor, under the name gpp forks  gpp_cc1plus   GCC 2.x C++ pass, under the name gpp forks  gpp_collect   GCC 2.x collect2, under the name gpp forks  cc1plus        a GCC C++ compiler pass (1.40.3) in the GCC2 directory; the                 driver runs it  cc2            the GCC 2.x C compiler pass, in the GCC2 directory; the                 driver runs it, and -version reports it  cc2plus        a second GCC C++ compiler pass (2.5.8) in the GCC2 directory  cccp2          the GCC 2.x preprocessor, in the GCC2 directory beside its                 driver  collect        collect2: builds the table of global constructors and                 destructors a C++ program needs before linkinggcc compiles to assembly and then needs r68, l68, clibn.l and cstart.r,Microware's.  With your SDK, both compilers work -- C and C++, compiled,linked and run.Where a compile reaches, on a four-line hello.c:  cccp2 file.c file.i        preprocesses.  Works.  gcc2 -S -o file.a file.c   compiles to 68k assembly.  Works, and the                             assembly is Microware's syntax -- `psect',                             `link.w a5,#0', `bsr _stkcheck'.  gcc2 -o file file.c        stops: `Can't fork \'r68\': 000:221 module                             not found'.So everything up to the assembler is here and works; the assembler andlinker are Microware's.  GCC139/gcc forks a module named `os9exec' andfails earlier -- use gcc2.  GCC139 (6) gcc gcc_cc1 gcc_cc1plus gcc_cccp gcc_collect gpp             Use CMDS/makeinfo (GCC139/makeinfo was the same file).  GCC2   (8) cc1plus cc2 cc2plus cccp2 collect gcc gcc2 gpp------------------------------------------------------/dd/CMDS/GCC137 -- GNU C 1.37.1, the CERN distribution (3)------------------------------------------------------GNU C 1.37.1 for OS-9/68000 as CERN distributed it in February 1991, withits source in SRC/gcc-1.37.1-osk.  It compiles for the 68000 and for the68020 with or without a 68881, and hands its assembly to your own r68 andl68.  Its headers are in /dd/GCCDEFS and its gnulib.l in /dd/GCCLIB,where it looks for them.  -B names where the passes are:    gcc137 -B/dd/CMDS/GCC137/gcc137_ -I/h1/DEFS -L/dd/GCCLIB -L/h1/LIB -o hello hello.cIt asks for a resident `cc1' and `cccp' before it looks where -B points,and GCC139's passes go by those names and stay resident once run: afterusing GCC139, `unlink cc1 cccp' first.  DOC/gcc137 has the distribution'sREADME, the port's notes (Doc), the history and the manuals. *gcc137        the GCC 1.37.1 driver: `gcc version 1.37.1' *gcc137_cccp   its preprocessor *gcc137_cc1    its C compiler pass: `GNU C version 1.37.1 (OS-9/68000)'------------------------------------------------------/dd/CMDS/GCC272 -- GNU C 2.7.2 (5)------------------------------------------------------GNU C and C++ 2.7.2 for OS-9/68000, ported in 1995-97 -- the newest gcc onthis disk.  Its source, as far as the archive carries it, is inSRC/gccsrc272; SRC/gccsrc272/README.OSK says what is missing.  It compilesfor the 68000 through the 68060 and CPU32, and hands its assembly to yourown r68 and l68.  Its support library is GNULIB/libgcc000.l (020 and 300for the other CPUs); -uwlibs links it with your own clibn.l.The driver forks its passes by bare name, so each is pointed at its ownfile with the driver's -F option, and GCC2_OPTS holds default options:    GCC2_OPTS="-Fcccp2x[/dd/CMDS/GCC272/gcc272_cccp2] -Fcc2x[/dd/CMDS/GCC272/gcc272_cc2] -Fcc2plusx[/dd/CMDS/GCC272/gcc272_cc2plus] -Fcollectx[/dd/CMDS/GCC272/gcc272_collect]"    gcc272 -uwlibs -L/h1/LIB -o hello hello.cC and C++ both compile, link and run; C++ programs link without a streamlibrary.  DOC/gcc272/readme.osk is the porter's ownguide to the options and search rules.  gcc272         the driver: `gcc2 version 2.7.2'  gcc272_cccp2   the preprocessor, for C and C++  gcc272_cc2     the C compiler pass: `GNU C version 2.7.2'  gcc272_cc2plus  the C++ compiler pass: `GNU C++ version 2.7.2' *gcc272_collect  builds the table of global constructors and destructors                 a C++ program needs; gcc272 runs it for every link------------------------------------------------------/dd/CMDS/DRIVERS -- drivers and file managers for a real system (10)------------------------------------------------------  Modules for an OS-9/68000 kernel: file managers, drivers, device  descriptors, and a cache utility with its trap handler.  You load them,  or put them in a bootfile; they are not programs, and started from a  shell they say so.  WE CANNOT TEST ANY OF THESE -- the emulator this  collection is checked on loads no file managers or drivers -- so what  is written here comes from their own documents and code.  Each has its  source in SRC.  ptyman         PtyMan 1.3, a pseudo-terminal file manager: one program                 opens /pty/<name> and gets the terminal end, another opens                 /tty/<name> and gets a line-editing, echoing terminal                 connected to it.  Any number of pairs, by name.  This is                 what a window or session multiplexer, or a network login                 server, is built on -- osknet's telnet server asks for                 /pty.  Its descriptors are pty (the server side, /pty) and                 tty (the client side, /tty -- a module, not the command                 of that name).  DOC/ptyman/ptyman.doc  ptydrv         PtyMan's placeholder driver; its descriptors name it  pty            the descriptor for PtyMan's server side, /pty  lfcrman        a file manager that shows another device with its line                 endings translated between LF and CR, for sharing text                 with a Unix machine over NFS.  From OS-9 International                 2/93  h0t            an example lfcrman descriptor: /h0t is /h0, translated  thlpct         another: /thlpct is /thlpc, translated  disp           an SCF driver written in C, the example from OS-9                 International 1/93, "Writing a device driver in C": it                 puts the low four bits of each character on a display                 port.  An example to adapt  di             its descriptor, port $FEC30000 *cpucache       shows and switches the 68020/68030/68040 on-chip caches.                 Its driver is not here, so of what it does only `cpucache                 -q on|off' works without one of your own  cctrap         cpucache's trap handler------------------------------------------------------/dd/CMDS/X68K -- programs for the Sharp X68000's window system (2)------------------------------------------------------  OS-9/X68000 ran on Sharp's X68000 with a windowing system of its own,  the personal window, reached through /win.  WE CANNOT TEST THESE:  there is no X68000 here, and each stops at the window it cannot open.  DOC/README-X68K has what their Japanese documents say; the archives  themselves ship whole in CMDS/archives, as their terms require. *x9eyes         xeyes for the personal window, resizable *setbgptn       sets the window system's background pattern from a file------------------------------------------------------/dd/CMDS/SYSADMIN -- logins, messages, modems, cron and an error log (15)------------------------------------------------------  System programs from TOP, The OS-9 Project's second release (Munich,  1989-90), for an OS-9 machine that several people log into, some of  them over modems.  WE CANNOT TEST WHAT THEY ARE FOR: that needs serial  lines, users and a password file, and users logged in at once, whom  logon records in SYS/utmp.  Most answer -? with their usage, which is  where most of their documentation lives; DOC/SYSADMIN has manuals for  logon, mmon, watch, mesg, mmenu, msg, newgrp, speak and uid in proff  format, utmp.prf on the SYS/utmp routines, and a sample entrytypes and  mmon.config. *logon          logs a user in: checks the password file, sets the user,                 the environment and the motd; -l keeps SYS/wtmp  mmon           a timesharing monitor for a terminal or Hayes-modem line:                 answers, sets the speed and runs logon, as configured in                 SYS/mmon.config *watch          watches a modem line for RING and starts a command on an                 incoming call.  Super-user only *timeout        logon's idle timer (logon -p says where to find it).  It                 prints no usage  errlog         a daemon that collects error messages into one file,                 SYS/errorlog on /h0  erradm         starts, stops, flushes and prints errlog's log *vcron          the cron daemon: runs each user's crontab.  Only members                 of group Cron may start 